# ============================ PAYROLL 7 ============================
LESSONS_P7 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The exit: payroll's closing procedure", "11", "<p>This module closes the employment relationship from the payroll side: the exit's records, the final slip's proration, leave encashment, the settlement computation and its document, the closure of loans and advances, the release checklist, and the special cases. The exit is where every earlier module's records are read one last time — and where every earlier shortcut presents its bill.</p><p><b>The exit's shape.</b> An exit is a decision (resignation accepted, contract ended, termination decided — HR's and the owner's territory, with professional advice where the case needs it) executed as records (this module's territory). The custody split holds to the end: the operator computes and documents what the records and the decisions say; the operator decides nothing about the exit itself.</p><p><b>The records that start it.</b> Two fields on the Employee record, set by HR the day the exit is confirmed:</p><p>1. <b>Relieving Date</b> — the last day of service. Every computation in this module hangs on it: the final month's proration, the encashment cutoff, the settlement date.<br>2. <b>Status</b> — moves to Left effective the relieving date, which is what removes the employee from future runs (module 3's Get Employees selects by it — the leaver who keeps getting paid is module 3's Pattern 2, and this field is its fix).</p><p><b>The exit's two payment shapes.</b> The business chooses per case, on the checklist:</p><p>1. <b>Inside the final regular run:</b> the relieving date falls in the period; the run prorates the final slip automatically; the settlement items (encashment, recoveries) enter as documents before the close and ride the same run. The default where dates allow — one run, one posting, one payment, all the standing controls applying.<br>2. <b>The off-cycle settlement:</b> the exit that cannot wait (an immediate departure, a mid-month termination the business chooses to settle at once) — module 3, Chapter 7's off-cycle lane: a documented slip and settlement, posted on the map, listed in the month's close. Off-cycle is the exception with its lane, never the improvised transfer.</p><p><b>What the exit reads from earlier modules.</b> The final computation touches everything: the assignment (the base the last month computes on), the payment-days engine (module 1 — the proration), the statutory set (module 2 — final pay is taxed and pensioned normally), the variable machinery (module 5 — encashment and recoveries enter as documents), the loans and advances registers (module 5's monthly reads deciding whether this exit is routine arithmetic or a recovery problem), and the sysadmin track's leaver procedure (access ending the same day — Chapter 7's cross-track line).</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the final slip. Chapter 3: leave encashment. Chapter 4: the settlement computation and its document. Chapter 5: closing loans and advances. Chapter 6: the release checklist. Chapter 7: the special cases. Chapter 8: the HealthTrade exits. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The final slip", "12", "<p>The leaver's last Salary Slip computes like any other — by the records — with the Relieving Date doing the work.</p><p><b>The proration.</b> The payment-days engine (module 1, Chapter 6) reads the Relieving Date exactly as it reads the Date of Joining in reverse: a relieving date of the 12th gives payment days from the period's start to the 12th; the components with <b>Depends on Payment Days</b> pay their fraction; flat components pay per their flags. No manual arithmetic — the operator's job is verification, not computation:</p><p>1. The Employee record's Relieving Date against the accepted resignation or the decision letter — the date is the decision's, entered exactly.<br>2. The final slip <b>previewed</b> (module 1, Chapter 7's procedure — the exit is a standing preview trigger): payment days against the calendar, each line's fraction sensible, the statutory lines present.</p><p><b>The statutory lines on final pay.</b> Final pay is pay: PAYE computes through the slab on the final taxable income, the pension family computes on the final B/H/T fractions, NHF per its rule — nothing switches off because someone is leaving. Two exit-specific notes for the accountant's page:</p><p>• <b>The encashment's tax treatment</b> (Chapter 3) is ruled separately — encashment is its own kind of payment and the ruling says how it taxes.<br>• <b>The remittance continues past the person:</b> the leaver's final PAYE joins the month's state schedule, their final pension joins their PFA's schedule — the custody chain (module 2) does not care that the employment ended, and the leaver's PFA remittance in particular is the one most often dropped, because the person is gone before the schedule runs. The checklist (Chapter 6) carries the line.</p><p><b>Notice-period mechanics, from the payroll side.</b> The contract's notice terms are HR's to apply; payroll executes the outcome as records:</p><p>• <b>Notice worked:</b> the relieving date is the notice's end; the final slip prorates to it. Nothing special.<br>• <b>Notice paid in lieu:</b> the payment-in-lieu is a settlement item — computed per the contract's terms on the sheet (Chapter 4), entered as a documented earning, taxed per the ruling.<br>• <b>Notice owed by the employee</b> (leaving short of contractual notice, where the contract provides for recovery): a settlement deduction — <b>with the same written-agreement discipline as every recovery</b> (module 5, Chapter 5): the signed acknowledgment in the exit paperwork, the amount per the contract's formula, on the sheet. Disputed notice recoveries route to the owner; the operator computes both versions and executes the decision.</p><p>Practical rule: the final slip is previewed <b>with the exit file open</b> — the resignation/decision letter, the contract's notice terms, the leave balance (Chapter 3), the loans and advances pages (Chapter 5). The exit preview is the one preview that reads five documents, because the final slip is the one slip that answers to all of them.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Leave encashment", "12", "<p>Where the leave policy pays out unused leave at exit, the platform computes and documents it through its own record.</p><p><b>The prerequisites, in order.</b></p><p>1. <b>The policy line:</b> which leave types encash (annual leave commonly; sick leave commonly not), at what rate basis, and any caps — the HR policy's written line, because encashment is a term of employment, not an operator's kindness.<br>2. <b>The leave balance, trustworthy:</b> the encashment computes from the leave machinery's balance at the relieving date — allocations minus approved applications (the HR track's records). The exit is where a year of casual leave-keeping presents its bill: a balance nobody maintained becomes a negotiation; a maintained balance is a lookup. The operator verifies the balance <b>with the employee</b> before computing — the leaver confirming their own balance in writing at exit is the cheapest dispute prevention in this module.<br>3. <b>The rate basis, configured:</b> the encashment amount per day derives from the salary structure per the policy's basis (the structure's encashment configuration names the component basis — commonly the Basic-derived daily rate). Set once with the accountant when the structure is built; verified at the first encashment.</p><p><b>The Leave Encashment record.</b> Open <b>Leave Encashment</b> (awesome bar) &gt; New: the employee, the leave type, the leave period, and the balance pulled from the leave records; the record computes <i>encashable days × the per-day rate</i> and, submitted, creates the encashment's entry into the pay machinery — landing on the final slip as its own named line. The record is the encashment's document: the balance, the rate, the arithmetic, and the approval, in one place, attached to the exit file.</p><p><b>The tax line.</b> The encashment's tax treatment is the accountant's ruling (Chapter 2's note): the component that carries encashment holds the ruled flag, and material encashments take the Deduct Full Tax policy consideration like any material one-off (module 5, Chapter 4's consistency rule). The operator's job is the standing one: the ruling on the page, the flag matching it, the plausibility visible on the previewed final slip.</p><p><b>The verification, on the sheet.</b> The settlement sheet (Chapter 4) carries the encashment as a line with its own working: <i>balance [n] days (confirmed by employee, date) × rate [basis] = amount, per Leave Encashment [record]</i> — one line that an auditor, the employee, and next year's operator can each follow without a meeting.</p><p>Practical rule: run the balance confirmation at the exit's <b>start</b>, not its end — the resignation acceptance meeting is the moment to hand the employee their leave statement and get the confirmation signed. A balance disputed on the last day delays the settlement; a balance confirmed on the first day makes the encashment arithmetic.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The settlement computation and its document", "13", "<p>The settlement is the exit's one number — everything owed each way, netted, documented, paid. This chapter is the sheet and the record.</p><p><b>The settlement sheet.</b> One page per leaver, built from the exit file, in two columns:</p><p><b>Owed to the employee:</b></p><p>1. The final slip's net (Chapter 2's prorated month — the sheet references the slip rather than recomputing it).<br>2. Leave encashment (Chapter 3's line, with its record).<br>3. Payment in lieu of notice, where the case has it (Chapter 2, per the contract).<br>4. Any other contractual or agreed items (the owed expense claim, the agreed terminal benefit per policy) — each with its document.</p><p><b>Owed by the employee:</b></p><p>5. Loan balance outstanding (Chapter 5, from the borrower's page).<br>6. Advance balances unsettled (Chapter 5).<br>7. Notice recovery, where the case has it (Chapter 2, agreed).<br>8. Agreed recoveries in flight (module 5's schedules — the remaining balance falls due at exit per the standard agreement wording, which the agreement template says explicitly for exactly this moment).<br>9. Company property unreturned <b>only as a last resort and only per signed terms</b> — the property checklist (Chapter 6) exists so this line stays empty.</p><p><b>The net, and its sign.</b> The columns net to the settlement figure. Positive: the business pays the leaver. Negative — the recoveries exceed the final pay — Chapter 5's escalation: the shortfall is the owner's decision on the record (repayment agreement, or the write-off through the Accounts track's two-signature lane), never a figure quietly absorbed or informally pursued.</p><p><b>The Full and Final Statement.</b> The platform carries the settlement as its own document — the <b>Full and Final Statement</b> record: the employee, the relieving date, the payables and receivables assembled (the settlement sheet's two columns as structured rows), the asset-return status, and the resulting net. Where the site uses it, the record <b>is</b> the sheet's system form — created at the exit's start, completed as the items confirm, and standing as the settlement's document of record with the sheet's sources attached. Either form — the record, or the signed sheet where the business runs on paper — the requirements are identical: every line documented, the net computed once, and both parties' sign-off on the final figure.</p><p><b>The payment.</b> The settlement pays like every payroll payment: through the books (the final run's payment, or the off-cycle Payment Entry referencing the settlement document), under the separation (preparer and releaser distinct — the leaver's settlement is the payment most worth a second pair of eyes, because it is the one payment with no next month to catch an error), to the verified bank account on the Employee record.</p><p><b>The sign-off.</b> The leaver signs the settlement — the figure, the items, the acknowledgment that it is full and final per its terms (the wording is the owner's/adviser's; the operator ensures the signature exists before release). The signed settlement plus its attached documents is the exit's answer to every future claim, which is why the release checklist (Chapter 6) will not pay without it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Closing loans, advances, and recoveries", "12", "<p>The leaver's open balances — the module 5 registers, read one last time and closed on documents.</p><p><b>The reads, at exit confirmation.</b> The day the exit is confirmed, three lookups from the standing pages:</p><p>1. <b>The loan balance</b> — the borrower's page (module 5, Chapter 7): outstanding principal, schedule position. The monthly reconciliation's payoff is exactly this moment: the balance is a known number, not an investigation.<br>2. <b>The advance balances</b> — the by-person reconciliation (module 5, Chapter 6): anything unsettled, of either kind (the salary advance mid-recovery; the expense advance awaiting its claim — chased now, because the claim that does not arrive before the settlement becomes a recovery line).<br>3. <b>The recoveries in flight</b> — active recurring deductions and their remaining balances from the run-down register.</p><p><b>The closure arithmetic.</b> Each balance lands on the settlement sheet's owed-by column at its exit-date figure. The standard agreement wording (module 5's templates) makes the mechanics automatic: outstanding balances fall due at exit and settle from the final entitlements — which is why the agreements were written that way at issue, for exactly this day.</p><p><b>The sufficient case.</b> Final entitlements cover the balances: the settlement nets positive, the deductions document on the sheet (and enter the final slip or the settlement record per the payment shape), the loan and advance records close, the pages note the closure. Routine arithmetic.</p><p><b>The insufficient case.</b> The balances exceed the entitlements — the settlement nets negative. The lane, in order:</p><p>1. <b>The number is established on the sheet</b> — both columns documented, the shortfall exact. No negotiation happens around a soft number.<br>2. <b>The owner decides, on the record:</b> a post-exit repayment agreement (schedule, signed — the leaver is now a debtor, and the agreement is what makes the debt collectable), or the write-off through the Accounts track's lane (the two-signature rule, the named approval, the posting to the write-off's account — a shortfall absorbed is an expense, and expenses are decided, not accreted).<br>3. <b>The operator executes and files:</b> the agreement or the write-off attached to the settlement; the loan/advance records closed with their resolution noted; the incident line where the case exposes a process gap (the loan whose affordability arithmetic was skipped, the advance that stalled unreconciled for months — the cause read feeds module 5's upstream fixes).</p><p><b>The prevention, restated once.</b> This chapter is easy or hard in proportion to module 5's monthly discipline: affordability checked at approval keeps balances proportionate to final entitlements; the monthly by-person reconciliation keeps every balance current and chased; the issue-day recovery rule keeps advances shrinking from day one. The exit that finds a two-year stale advance is not an exit problem — it is twenty-four skipped monthly reads presenting together.</p><p>Practical rule: the three lookups happen the day the exit confirms, and their figures go on the sheet the same day — the settlement conversation that starts with known numbers stays a conversation; the one that starts with an investigation becomes a dispute.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The release checklist", "12", "<p>The settlement releases against a checklist — one page, every line initialled, because the exit is the one payroll event with no next month to catch what was missed.</p><p><b>The checklist, in order.</b></p><p>1. <b>The records:</b> Relieving Date and Status set on the Employee record, matching the decision letter. (The final slip's correctness and the leaver's exclusion from future runs both hang here.)<br>2. <b>The final slip:</b> previewed with the exit file open (Chapter 2's five-document preview), the proration verified, statutory lines present.<br>3. <b>The leave balance:</b> confirmed with the employee in writing (Chapter 3's start-of-exit rule), the encashment record created and matching.<br>4. <b>The balances:</b> loans, advances, recoveries — the three lookups on the sheet at exit-date figures (Chapter 5), closures or the escalation documented.<br>5. <b>The settlement document:</b> the sheet or the Full and Final Statement complete, every line sourced, the net computed once, <b>the leaver's signature obtained</b>.<br>6. <b>Company property:</b> the property list cleared — devices, keys, cards, stock in custody per the assets register — signed off by the holder of each item's custody. The property line clears <b>before</b> release so the settlement never becomes the lever (and so Chapter 4's line 9 stays empty).<br>7. <b>Access, the same day:</b> the sysadmin track's leaver procedure — the User disabled on the relieving date, sessions revoked, the shared-credential rotations where any applied, self-service ended (the slips remain in the records; the login does not). The payroll operator does not perform this line; the payroll operator <b>confirms</b> it happened, because the leaver with live access is the finding both tracks named and neither track wants to own after the fact.<br>8. <b>The statutory tail:</b> the final month's remittances carry the leaver — the PAYE on the state schedule, the pension to their PFA (Chapter 2's most-dropped line, printed here so it cannot be), the certificates and records retained per the statutory periods.<br>9. <b>The exit documents issued:</b> the certificate of service and the exit paperwork per policy (HR issues; payroll confirms the settlement's copy is in the file), and the final payslip delivered through the standing confidential channel.<br>10. <b>The payment released</b> — under the separation, to the verified account, referencing the settlement document.</p><p><b>The file.</b> Everything above lands in one exit file per leaver: the decision letter, the confirmations, the encashment record, the sheet/statement signed, the closures, the property sign-offs, the payment reference. The file is the exit's single answer — to the leaver's future query, the auditor's sample, the authority's question — and it is complete on release day or the release waits.</p><p>Practical rule: the checklist is initialled as lines complete, not reconstructed at the end. An exit runs over days and hands; the live checklist is what keeps ten lines across three people from becoming nine lines and a surprise.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The special cases", "12", "<p>The exits that test the procedure. Each keeps the same machinery and changes the handling.</p><p><b>The immediate termination.</b> A dismissal effective at once: the decision and its process are the owner's, with professional advice — misconduct terminations in particular carry legal requirements that precede any payroll action, and the operator's discipline is to <b>wait for the documented decision</b>: no settlement computes from a corridor account of a dismissal. Once documented: the off-cycle shape (Chapter 1), the settlement per the decision's terms (what is paid and what is withheld in a misconduct case is the decision's content, not the operator's judgment), the checklist unabridged — access closure the same day matters most in exactly this case, and the property and settlement lines proceed in whatever order the decision directs.</p><p><b>Death in service.</b> The hardest case, handled with its weight:</p><p>1. <b>The tone:</b> the family's contact runs through the owner/HR, once, with care — payroll's arithmetic never leads the conversation.<br>2. <b>The machinery:</b> the Employee record closes with the date and the appropriate status; the final computation runs the standard sheet — final pay, encashment, entitlements per policy and any group-life/benefits terms (the accountant and adviser rule the items and their treatment).<br>3. <b>The payment:</b> to the estate or the named beneficiaries <b>per legal advice</b> — never simply to the account on file, because the account's ownership has legally changed; this is the one settlement where the verified-account rule yields to the adviser's instruction, documented.<br>4. <b>The balances:</b> outstanding loans and advances in a death case are the owner's decision with advice (policies and group cover often address them); the operator documents the resolution, whatever it is.</p><p><b>The disputed exit.</b> Where the exit itself is contested (the resignation retracted, the termination challenged), payroll <b>freezes at the documented state</b>: the records reflect the last documented decision; computations wait for resolution; interim instructions come from the owner in writing. The operator who executes one side's account of a dispute has joined it.</p><p><b>The abandonment.</b> The employee who stops appearing: HR's process runs first (the contacts, the notices, the contractual abandonment terms); payroll's part is the honest interim — unmarked absence reduces payment days per the standing engine (the records tell the truth while the process runs), and the exit computes on the process's documented conclusion and its effective date.</p><p><b>The retirement.</b> The planned exit at its best: months of notice, the balances wound down on schedule (the loan's term set at approval to clear before the date — module 5's affordability arithmetic, time-shaped), the encashment and entitlements computed without pressure, the pension handover to the PFA confirmed. The retirement checklist is Chapter 6's plus the pension-transition line — and a retirement executed cleanly is the exit procedure's best advertisement to the staff who watched it.</p><p>Practical rule: every special case shares one operator's rule — <b>the machinery computes; the decisions come documented from their owners.</b> The cases differ in weight and wording; the discipline does not move.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: two HealthTrade exits", "13", "<p>The procedure run twice — once routine, once hard. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>Exit one — the resignation, end to end.</b> A Head Office accounts assistant resigns with full contractual notice; the relieving date lands on a month-end (the final-regular-run shape, Chapter 1). Day one: HR sets the Relieving Date and Status; the operator runs the three balance lookups — no loan, no advance, one recovery in flight (a staff purchase, two instalments remaining) — and hands her the leave statement at the acceptance meeting: <b>nine days</b> unused annual leave, confirmed and signed same day (Chapter 3's start-of-exit rule). The Leave Encashment record computes the nine days at the structure's ruled basis; the settlement sheet builds: final month's net (full month — the date is month-end, no proration), plus encashment, minus the recovery's remaining balance (the agreement's falls-due-at-exit wording doing its job). The Full and Final Statement assembles the columns; she signs on her last day. The checklist walks: property cleared (laptop, keys), <b>access disabled the same evening — confirmed with the sysadmin, initialled</b>, the final slip delivered encrypted, payment released by the MD against the signed statement. The statutory tail runs with the month: her final PAYE on the Lagos schedule, her final pension on her PFA's schedule — the checklist's line 8 ticked with the schedules attached. File closed in eleven lines, zero disputes.</p><p><b>Exit two — the termination with the negative settlement.</b> A driver is dismissed mid-month following the owner's documented disciplinary process (the operator waited for the decision letter — Chapter 7's rule, held under pressure to \"just stop his pay\"). The off-cycle shape applies. The lookups find the hard fact: a staff loan with <b>a balance exceeding the final entitlements</b> — the final prorated pay plus four days' encashment cover most, not all, of it. The sheet establishes the exact shortfall (Chapter 5's rule: the number first); the MD decides on the record: a post-exit repayment agreement for the shortfall, signed at the settlement meeting, three monthly payments. The settlement statement documents both columns, the net, and the agreement; the checklist runs unabridged — access closed the same day (the case where it matters most), property recovered before the meeting, the statutory tail carried. The incident line does its upstream work: the loan pre-dated the affordability arithmetic (approved in the spreadsheet era at a term the salary could not safely carry), and the cause read's note to the MD closes the loop: <i>every loan approved since the policy page carries the arithmetic; this one taught us why.</i> The payroll log's quarter note: <i>two exits, one easy, one hard — both closed on documents, both answerable from one file each.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the seventh law", "11", "<p>The exit layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the leaver who keeps getting paid.</b> Symptom: salary credits after the last day; the recovery conversation with someone who no longer answers. Cause: Relieving Date and Status not set the day the exit confirmed (Chapters 1-2). Fix: the two fields as the exit's first act, the run's headcount reconciliation naming every leaver (module 3's equation working as designed), and the checklist's line 1.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the negotiated balance.</b> Symptom: leave balances and loan figures argued at exit; settlements delayed by investigations; numbers agreed by fatigue. Cause: the registers not maintained monthly (Chapters 3, 5). Fix: the standing reads that make every balance a lookup — and at the exit, the day-one lookups and the start-of-exit leave confirmation, so the settlement conversation opens with known numbers.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the settlement without a document.</b> Symptom: a final figure paid from a conversation; no signed statement; the claim arriving a year later with nothing to answer it. Cause: the sheet and sign-off skipped (Chapter 4). Fix: the settlement sheet or Full and Final Statement with every line sourced, the leaver's signature before release, and the checklist refusing payment without it.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the lever settlement.</b> Symptom: the settlement withheld to force property returns or concessions beyond its terms; the dispute the withholding creates outcosting the item it chased. Cause: the property line run after release instead of before, or the settlement used as pressure (Chapter 6). Fix: the checklist's order — property clears before release day arrives — and disputes routed to the owner as decisions, not exercised through the operator's timing.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the dropped statutory tail.</b> Symptom: the leaver's final pension never reaching their PFA; the query arriving months later from the fund or the person; the remittance schedule missing the name that was \"already gone\". Cause: the tail unowned (Chapters 2, 6). Fix: the checklist's line 8 — the final month's schedules carrying the leaver, attached to the file — and module 2's schedules derived from the register, which still contains the final slip.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the live login.</b> Symptom: the leaver's access working weeks after the exit; the finding both tracks named. Cause: the access line assumed rather than confirmed (Chapter 6). Fix: same-day closure by the sysadmin's procedure, <b>confirmed and initialled</b> on the payroll checklist — two tracks, one line, no gap between them.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the exit's shape, the two fields that start it, and the two payment shapes; verify the final slip's proration with the five-document preview and carry the statutory lines including the tail; run the encashment from policy line to Leave Encashment record with the start-of-exit balance confirmation; build the settlement sheet's two columns, use the Full and Final Statement, and handle both signs of the net; close loans, advances, and recoveries with the insufficient case's owner-decision lane; walk the ten-line release checklist; and handle the special cases on the one operator's rule.</p><p><b>The seventh law: the exit closes on documents — the final slip prorated to the relieving date, the balances read the day the exit confirms, the settlement computed once and signed, the statutory tail carried, and the leaver's access ending the same day.</b> Module 8 closes the track: settings, controls, the payroll year, and the eight laws.</p>"),
]

