# ============================ PAYROLL 8 ============================
LESSONS_P8 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The structural close", "11", "<p>The final module assembles the function: the Payroll Settings walk completed, the control set gathered, the payroll year's calendar, structure versioning, the audit folder, multi-company payroll, the handover test, and the track's eight laws.</p><p><b>Where the track stands.</b> Modules 1-7 built the machinery and its procedures: the records that compute pay, the statutory layer, the run, the slip, the variable lanes, the change governance, the exit. Each module left behind artifacts — registers, checklists, calendars, policy pages. This module's work is <b>structure</b>: making the collection into a function that runs the same regardless of who runs it — the programme's standing test (the Accounts and Sysadmin tracks each closed on it), now applied to payroll.</p><p><b>The three structural claims, previewed.</b></p><p>1. <b>The settings are written decisions</b> (Chapter 2): every behaviour switch on the Payroll Settings page walked, decided, and recorded — because an unexamined setting is a decision someone made by not making it, computing every slip in the company.<br>2. <b>The controls are layered, not personal</b> (Chapter 3): the separations, reconciliations, registers, and calendars gathered onto one page — visible as a system, auditable as a system, and survivable as a system.<br>3. <b>The function is answerable from its folder</b> (Chapters 5-7): the payroll year closes on its calendar, the audit answers from one place, and the handover test proves the operator is replaceable — which is the operator's professional achievement, not their diminishment: the function that depends on its holder is a risk; the function that survives its holder is an asset, and building the second kind is what this certification certifies.</p><p><b>The custody split, one last time.</b> The track's standing division closes where it opened: HR owns who and on what terms; the accountant owns the numbers' policy; the owner owns the decisions; <b>the operator owns the machinery and its evidence</b> — and this module is the machinery's own record: how it is configured, controlled, closed, and handed over. Every chapter that follows produces one more page of that record, and the record's completeness is what Chapters 6 and 7 will test.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Payroll Settings walk. Chapter 3: the control set. Chapter 4: structure versioning. Chapter 5: the payroll year. Chapter 6: the audit folder. Chapter 7: multi-company payroll and the handover test. Chapter 8: the HealthTrade year close. Chapter 9: the eight laws.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Payroll Settings walk", "13", "<p>Open <b>Payroll Settings</b> (awesome bar). Module 4 walked the delivery flags; this chapter completes the page — every field read, decided with its owner, and recorded on the settings page (the sysadmin track's walk rule: the walk is the routine occasion for change; between walks the page is stable).</p><p><b>The computation block — decided with the accountant, because each switch changes every proration in the company.</b></p><p>1. <b>The payroll basis</b> — whether payment days derive from leave records or from attendance marking. The choice matches the business's actual discipline: a business that marks attendance daily can compute from attendance; one that manages by exception computes from leave records (absences entered as leave of the right type). The decision is honest about which records the business genuinely maintains — computing from records nobody keeps produces slips nobody trusts.<br>2. <b>Unmarked attendance handling</b> — where attendance-based computation runs: what an unmarked day means (present or absent). The default posture and its consequence stated on the page: treating unmarked as absent enforces marking discipline sharply (and shorts the slip of everyone the marking missed); treating it as present is forgiving (and pays the absence the marking missed). Decided knowingly, with the HR track's attendance procedures as the counterpart.<br>3. <b>Holidays in total working days</b> — whether the denominator counts holidays. The switch changes every daily rate and every proration fraction in the company; it is set once with the accountant, recorded with a worked example on the page (one mid-month joiner computed both ways, so the decision is visible as arithmetic), and never changed casually — a mid-year flip makes two halves of the year prorate differently, which is a finding factory.</p><p><b>The presentation block.</b></p><p>4. <b>Rounded totals</b> — whether net pay rounds. Decided with the accountant (the rounding difference posts per the platform's handling); the slip and the bank file must agree either way.<br>5. <b>Leave balances on the slip</b> — showing each employee their balance on every slip. Recommended on where the leave records are maintained (module 7's start-of-exit confirmation becomes a formality when every slip has been showing the balance all year); left off where the balances would embarrass the record-keeping — and that condition, written on the page, is its own prompt to fix the records.</p><p><b>The posting block — decided with the accountant.</b></p><p>6. <b>Posting granularity</b> — whether the run's accrual posts per employee or consolidated. Consolidated keeps the General Ledger compact; per-employee makes the ledger itself answer person-level questions (at the cost of volume). The decision follows the business's reading habits — module 2's register-derivations pattern already answers person-level questions from the register, so consolidated is the common default — and the posting-read (module 2, Chapter 6) verifies whichever shape was chosen.<br>7. <b>The delivery flags</b> — module 4's territory, referenced: email delivery on, encryption on, the password scheme's line.</p><p>Practical rule: the settings page's record carries, per field: the value, the decision's owner, the date, and the one-line reason. Fifteen minutes at the walk; the alternative is the archaeology every unexplained setting eventually demands.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The control set, assembled", "12", "<p>The track's controls, gathered from their modules onto one page — the payroll function's control register, reviewed annually.</p><p><b>The separations.</b></p><p>1. Preparer never releases — the run's payment and every remittance (modules 2-3).<br>2. The decision/execution split — no record without its approved, evidenced decision (module 6's matrix; module 5's approvals; module 7's exit decisions).<br>3. The reviewer distinct from the preparer at Station 3, where staffing allows (module 3).</p><p><b>The reconciliations, by cadence.</b></p><p>• <b>Per run:</b> the headcount equation; the register comparison; the posting tie; the three-way payment total (module 3).<br>• <b>Monthly:</b> the custody zeros after remittance (module 2); the advances and loans by person (module 5); the delivery log's bounces closed (module 4).<br>• <b>Quarterly:</b> the movement record to the assignment list — the naked-assignment sweep (module 6); the payroll access recertification riding the sysadmin cycle (module 4); the cause reads — corrections, queries, arrears counts (modules 3-6).<br>• <b>Annually:</b> the slab and applicability re-reads (module 2); the settings walk (Chapter 2); the control register itself.</p><p><b>The registers.</b> The component register with its rulings (module 1); the applicability page (module 2); the movement record (module 6); the variable-pay register with its run-downs (module 5); the borrower and advance pages (module 5); the exit files (module 7); the settings record (Chapter 2).</p><p><b>The calendars.</b> The run's five stations on their dates (module 3); the remittance calendar with the pension window pinned to the payment date (module 2); the review round's timetable (module 6); the annual lines above.</p><p><b>The control register's use.</b> One page listing every control with its owner, cadence, and evidence location. Three readers use it:</p><p>1. <b>The operator</b> — the working map; nothing runs from memory.<br>2. <b>The reviewer/owner</b> — the oversight view: sampling any control's evidence without asking anyone where it lives.<br>3. <b>The auditor</b> — Chapter 6's visit starts from this page.</p><p><b>The layering logic, stated once.</b> No single control is trusted alone: the headcount equation catches what the close missed; the register comparison catches what the equation cannot see; the posting tie catches the wiring; the releaser's read catches what every earlier layer somehow passed; the quarterly sweeps catch what the runs normalised. The layers are cheap individually — seconds to minutes — and their value is the property that a failure must pass all of them, which honest errors and dishonest ones alike find hard.</p><p>Practical rule: when any control finds nothing for a year, the annual review asks whether it is redundant or working — and the answer is almost always <i>working</i>: the control that finds nothing is usually the control whose existence prevents what it would find. Retire controls rarely and deliberately.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Structure versioning", "12", "<p>Module 6's Chapter 7 routed the structure redesign here. This chapter is the discipline for changing the pay machinery itself.</p><p><b>What counts as a structural change.</b> Anything that alters how a package computes for everyone on it: the B/H/T split moved, a component added to or removed from a structure, a structure-row formula changed, a component's own formula or flag changed (the tax flag ruling revised, a rate updated on the accountant's table), a new Income Tax Slab at a reform. Individual bases are module 6's lane; the machinery is this one.</p><p><b>The versioning rule.</b> The machinery's history must stay readable: every past slip was computed by the records as they stood, and those records must remain visible as they stood. Hence:</p><p>1. <b>Slabs:</b> the module 2 rule verbatim — a new dated slab per reform, the old one never edited, attachment by new assignments.<br>2. <b>Structures:</b> a material redesign is a <b>new structure version</b> (the amended document or the successor structure, named with its effective period where the change is large enough to warrant it), with the affected employees moved by <b>new assignments from the effective date</b> — the supersession rule at batch scale. The old structure and old assignments stand as the record of what computed the past.<br>3. <b>Components:</b> flag and formula changes on a shared component change every structure that carries it — the impact list is written before the change (which structures, which employees, which statutory lines move), and where the old and new rules must coexist across a boundary date, the answer is a successor component or the structure-row override, never a component whose meaning silently changed mid-year.</p><p><b>The change procedure (the sysadmin track's eight steps, payroll-shaped).</b></p><p>1. The request written with its decision — owner and accountant together for anything touching the split or the statutory set (module 6's matrix row).<br>2. The impact list from the registers: structures, headcount, statutory consequences.<br>3. Built and <b>previewed per affected structure</b> in the test discipline — one slip per structure at a representative base, the plausibility check re-run where tax moved (module 2).<br>4. The before-change backup and the deployment window per the sysadmin rules.<br>5. The batch assignments entered and verified both ways (module 6's round procedure re-used).<br>6. The first run's register comparison expecting exactly the modelled movement — the change's own prediction tested against its result.<br>7. The registers updated: the component register's rulings, the settings record where a setting moved, the movement record's batch note.<br>8. The announcement — staff told what changes on their slips and why, before the slip tells them.</p><p>Practical rule: schedule structural changes to <b>period boundaries</b> — effective the 1st, entered before the close, live for a whole month's first run. A mid-period structural change makes one month compute under two regimes, which is possible but never worth it when a week's patience buys a clean boundary.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The payroll year", "12", "<p>The annual cycle — the lines that run once a year, on the calendar, closing one payroll year and opening the next.</p><p><b>The year-end block, in order.</b></p><p>1. <b>The statutory re-reads</b> (module 2's standing lines, scheduled here): the Income Tax Slab confirmed against current law with the accountant — a reform produces the new dated slab, attached by new assignments before the first run of the new year; the applicability page re-confirmed line by line; the threshold watch checked against the year's headcount.<br>2. <b>The annual tax reconciliation and certificates:</b> each employee's year — taxable income, PAYE withheld — reconciled (the register's YTD columns and the slip's YTD block carrying the figures), and the <b>annual PAYE certificate</b> issued per employee per the authority's requirements: the document the employee's own filing and the authority's cross-check both rely on. The certificates derive from the same closed registers as everything else — one source, one truth — and their issue is a calendar line with an owner and a date.<br>3. <b>The employer's annual returns:</b> the year's statutory filings per authority (the annual employer declarations that accompany the monthly remittance pattern) — the accountant's filings, prepared from the operator's registers and evidence bundles; the division of authority holding at year-end exactly as monthly.<br>4. <b>The year's registers closed:</b> the movement record's year read (changes, arrears count, off-cycle count — the decision-timing data to the owner); the variable-pay year totals; the loans and advances year positions; the exit files' completeness sweep.<br>5. <b>The opening balances for the new year</b> — where the machinery carries year-to-date figures forward or opens fresh (mid-year go-lives in particular enter their opening earnings and tax figures so the annualised tax computes correctly from day one), the opening entries are made from documented figures and verified on the first new-year preview.</p><p><b>The new-year block.</b></p><p>6. The annual review round on its timetable (module 6) — commonly adjacent to year-end, scheduled so the round and the year-close do not collide on the same close week.<br>7. The settings walk (Chapter 2) and the control-register review (Chapter 3).<br>8. The annual confidentiality read (module 4) and the payroll access recertification's annual summary.</p><p><b>The year file.</b> The year closes into one folder-of-folders: twelve run checklists and registers, twelve remittance evidence bundles, the certificates issued, the annual returns, the year's register closes, the settings and control pages as reviewed. Chapter 6's audit answers from this file; the operator's successor inherits it; the seven-year retention rule (the Accounts track's) governs its keeping.</p><p>Practical rule: the year-end block is scheduled in writing in month eleven — owners named per line, dates set clear of the December close's own pressure. A year-end improvised in the last week produces the one thing this track exists to prevent: numbers without documents, at the moment the most documents are due.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The payroll audit", "12", "<p>Payroll is audited — by the external auditor's payroll section, by statutory inspections, by the owner's own review. This chapter is answering all three from one place.</p><p><b>What the auditor asks, mapped to what already exists.</b></p><p>1. <b>\"Show me that everyone paid exists and is approved.\"</b> The run checklists' headcount reconciliations (every month's equation with its joiner/leaver names), the Employee records with their verification attachments, sampled assignments traced to their letters via the movement record. The ghost-employee question, answered by the controls that prevent it.<br>2. <b>\"Show me that pay matches its authority.\"</b> Sampled slips traced: the base to the assignment to the letter; the variable lines to their Additional Salary records with evidence and approvals; the structural rates to the component register's rulings.<br>3. <b>\"Show me the statutory chain.\"</b> Per sampled month: the register, its derived schedules, the Payment Entries, the receipts, the zero checks — module 2's evidence bundles, produced as filed. The slab against the law of its period; the certificates against the register's YTD.<br>4. <b>\"Show me the postings.\"</b> The posting ties on the checklists; the wiring map; the cost-center trail from the Employee records to the branch P&amp;L salary lines.<br>5. <b>\"Show me the exits.\"</b> Sampled exit files: the settlement's two columns sourced, the signature, the balance closures, the tail schedules.<br>6. <b>\"Who can do what?\"</b> The payroll access list from the recertifications; the separations demonstrated (who prepared, who released — the names differ, every month).</p><p><b>The audit posture.</b> Three rules for the visit:</p><p>1. <b>Answer from the file, not from memory</b> — every question above ends at a document, which is faster for the auditor and safer for the operator than any explanation.<br>2. <b>The finding is a gift:</b> an auditor's finding is a control gap found by a friendly reader on a schedule — it enters the incident log with its cause and produces its upstream fix exactly like an internal finding (the cause-read discipline, with an external trigger).<br>3. <b>Never improve the past:</b> the file presents as it was kept. A document \"tidied\" for the audit is worse than its original imperfection — the track's whole design is that the records were right when made, and the audit is where that design pays.</p><p><b>The internal audit rhythm.</b> The owner's own review — quarterly or annual per the business's appetite — samples the same six questions at smaller scale, from the control register's map. The internal read's purpose is the external one's rehearsal <b>and</b> the finding found first: the gap the internal sweep catches in March is a process note; the same gap in the external audit is a finding with an audience.</p><p>Practical rule: after the first audit, file the auditor's request list with the year file — next year's preparation is this year's list, and a function that answers the list from the standing file in one sitting has passed the only structural test an audit really runs.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Multi-company payroll and the handover test", "12", "<p>The track's last two structures: payroll across legal entities, and the proof that the function survives its operator.</p><p><b>Multi-company payroll.</b> Where the site carries more than one company, payroll respects the legal separation completely — the Accounts track's rule, executed in payroll's terms:</p><p>1. <b>Employment is per company:</b> each Employee record belongs to its employing entity; a person genuinely serving two entities is two employment records or a documented cross-charge arrangement (the accountant's design), never one salary informally split.<br>2. <b>The machinery is per company:</b> Salary Components carry per-company account rows (module 1's Accounts table — the same component posting to each company's own chart); structures and slabs attach per company; <b>the Payroll Entry runs per company</b> (module 3's one-run rule reads: one run per company per period).<br>3. <b>The statutory chain is per company:</b> each entity's registrations, schedules, remittances, and certificates in its own name — the custody accounts per company's chart, the evidence bundles per company's file.<br>4. <b>The registers split accordingly:</b> the year files, the control register's evidence locations, and the audit answers all resolve per entity — the auditor of one company sees one company.</p><p><b>The handover test.</b> The programme's standing structural test, applied to payroll: could a competent successor, given only the function's documentation, run next month's payroll correctly? The documentation the test reads is this track's artifact list — the settings record, the component register with rulings, the applicability page, the structures and the assignment register, the run checklist template and twelve filed examples, the remittance calendar and evidence bundles, the movement record, the variable-pay registers and policy pages, the exit checklist and files, the control register mapping all of it. The test is run for real:</p><p>1. <b>The absence test:</b> the backstop (a trained second — the accountant's payroll-literate colleague, or the deliberately cross-trained deputy) runs one full cycle on the documentation while the operator is on leave: the close, the run's five stations, the delivery, the remittance preparation. The operator answers no questions that the file should answer — and every question asked <b>is</b> a documentation finding, fixed on return.<br>2. <b>The annual confirmation:</b> the control register's review confirms the backstop's currency (procedures change; the backstop's training follows) and the documentation's completeness against the artifact list.</p><p><b>Why this is the certification's last word.</b> A payroll that lives in one person's head holds the business hostage to leave, illness, and departure — and holds the operator hostage to their own indispensability. The track's every module built the alternative: pay computed by records, controlled by layers, evidenced in files, and documented to the standard a stranger can run. The operator who builds that has built the professional asset this certification names.</p><p>Practical rule: run the first absence test <b>deliberately and soon</b> — a planned week with the operator reachable — rather than discovering the documentation's gaps during an unplanned absence with the operator unreachable. The rehearsal is cheap; the emergency premiere is not.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's year close", "13", "<p>The structural chapters, executed. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>The settings walk (Chapter 2).</b> Run with the accountant in one sitting: the payroll basis confirmed as leave-based (matching the discipline the business actually keeps — attendance marking is branch-patchy, and the page says so honestly as the reason); holidays-in-working-days recorded with the worked example (the mid-month joiner computed both ways on the page); rounded totals confirmed against the bank file's behaviour; leave balances switched <b>on</b> for slips — the leave records having earned it through the HR-side cleanup; posting granularity confirmed consolidated, with the register-derivations note as the reason. Seven fields, seven one-line reasons, one page.</p><p><b>The control register (Chapter 3).</b> Assembled from the modules' artifacts in an afternoon: nineteen controls, each with owner, cadence, and evidence location. The MD's oversight sampling begins the same quarter — two controls sampled unannounced, both evidenced in one lookup each.</p><p><b>A structural change through the lane (Chapter 4).</b> The accountant's annual table update moves a statutory rate. The impact list names all three structures; the change enters at a period boundary — effective the 1st, entered before the close — previewed per structure with the plausibility check re-run, the batch verified both ways, and the first run's comparison matching the modelled movement to the naira. Staff are told before the slips tell them: one paragraph with the payday note.</p><p><b>The year-end (Chapter 5).</b> Scheduled in writing in month eleven, owners named. The slab re-read confirms the current table; the applicability page re-confirms with one change — the threshold watch flags that the year's hiring has crossed a levy's headcount line, and the accountant registers the new obligation <b>before</b> its first due date rather than after its first penalty. The annual PAYE certificates issue to all staff from the closed registers; the employer's annual returns file from the same source; the year folder closes: twelve checklists, twelve bundles, the certificates, the returns, the register closes.</p><p><b>The audit (Chapter 6).</b> The external auditor's payroll section visits in the new year. The six questions run from the control register's map: the headcount equations sampled across four months, three slips traced letter-to-slip, one remittance month walked register-to-receipt-to-zero, the driver's exit file read end to end (the signed settlement and repayment agreement answering the hard case's every question). One finding: two months' delivery logs missing their bounce-closure notes — logged, caused (the log line's template lacked the field), fixed upstream. The auditor's request list files with the year folder.</p><p><b>The handover test (Chapter 7).</b> The deliberate absence: the operator takes a planned week mid-cycle; the trained backstop — the accounts assistant cross-trained across the quarter — runs the close, the five stations, the delivery, and the remittance preparation from the file. Two questions get asked; both become documentation fixes (the bank-file export's steps written down; the encashment rate basis's location noted on the exit checklist). The MD's year note: <i>payroll ran for a week without its operator, answered an audit from one folder, and caught a new statutory obligation before it caught us — the function is now a system.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the track's eight laws", "12", "<p>The structural layer's recurring failures, the eighth law, and the track assembled.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the unexamined settings.</b> Symptom: prorations nobody can explain; the holidays switch discovered mid-dispute; every slip computing on decisions nobody made. Cause: the Payroll Settings page never walked (Chapter 2). Fix: the walk with the accountant, the page's record — value, owner, date, reason — and the annual re-walk as the only change occasion.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the personal control set.</b> Symptom: controls that exist as one person's habits; oversight that must ask where evidence lives; the audit as archaeology. Cause: the controls never gathered (Chapter 3). Fix: the control register — every control with owner, cadence, evidence location — and the owner's unannounced sampling proving it real.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the mid-year drift.</b> Symptom: a component's meaning silently changed in July; two halves of the year computing differently; history unreadable. Cause: structural changes without versioning (Chapter 4). Fix: new dated slabs, structure versions with batch assignments, successor components across boundaries, changes at period boundaries — history standing, always.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the improvised year-end.</b> Symptom: certificates late, returns scrambled, the re-reads skipped in the December crush; the reform discovered by the first wrong run of January. Cause: no year calendar (Chapter 5). Fix: the year-end block scheduled in writing in month eleven, owners per line, clear of the close's own pressure.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the audit as performance.</b> Symptom: the visit preceded by weeks of tidying; documents improved; answers from memory and charm. Cause: the file not kept audit-ready as a working state (Chapter 6). Fix: answer from the file as it was kept, findings as gifts into the cause read, and the request list filed for next year — the audit passing because the year did.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the indispensable operator.</b> Symptom: payroll stops when one person is sick; the handover that would take months; the business hostage to a resignation. Cause: the function never structurally tested (Chapter 7). Fix: the artifact list complete, the backstop trained, the deliberate absence test run — and every question it surfaces fixed as the documentation finding it is.</p><p><b>The eighth law: payroll survives its operator — settings written, controls layered and registered, the machinery versioned, the year closed on its calendar, the audit answered from one folder, and the function proven by absence.</b></p><p><b>The track's eight laws.</b></p><p>1. Pay is computed by the records — components carry their accounts, structures the package, assignments the base, and every slip previews before it pays.<br>2. Statutory amounts are computed by components from the accountant's current table, posted to typed custody accounts, remitted by the calendar from the reserve, and zeroed every cycle.<br>3. The run is a procedure — records closed, drafts reconciled and read, posting tied before payment, payment separated from preparation, and the month closed on the checklist that proves it.<br>4. The slip is the employee's statement — computed by records, delivered one-to-one after the money moves, explained on request, and confidential everywhere else.<br>5. Variable pay enters as documents — approved before the close, computed from evidence, taxed by the flags and policy, and recovered only by written agreement on a self-ending schedule.<br>6. A pay change is a decision executed as a record — approved on the matrix, anchored to its letter, effective from its honest date, arrears by document, history standing.<br>7. The exit closes on documents — the final slip prorated, balances read day one, the settlement signed, the statutory tail carried, and access ending the same day.<br>8. Payroll survives its operator — settings written, controls layered, the year closed on its calendar, and the whole function answerable from one folder.</p><p>This completes the Payroll Professional track.</p>"),
]

